---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import "../../components/studio.css";
---

<Layout title="Admin - Guide" wordmark="admin" backdrop="guide">
  <main class="admin" id="main">
    <section id="sec-info" class="prose">
      <h2>Good to know</h2>
      <h3>Adding a painting</h3>
      <p>
        Tap “Add painting”, choose the photo, then give it a title, price and
        size. Daylight beats ceiling light: hang the painting near a window and
        photograph it straight on, filling the frame. That one photo also builds
        the “try it on your wall” preview, so a blurry photo means a blurry
        preview.
      </p>
      <p>
        Stuck for words? “Suggest a title and description” looks at the photo
        and offers a few titles and a description. Pick one, then change
        anything you like — nothing is used until you choose it.
      </p>
      <h3>Publishing</h3>
      <p>
        “Publish painting” puts it on the site within a few minutes — the site
        rebuilds itself after every save. “Save draft” keeps it in the studio
        only. Give a draft a “Publish on” date and it goes live by itself that
        day.
      </p>
      <p>
        Tick “On publish, email my list” and everyone on your list hears about
        it when it goes live — right away, or on its day if it's scheduled.
      </p>
      <h3>Putting the paintings in order</h3>
      <p>
        On the Collection page, drag the Available and Sold cards into whatever
        order you like — the homepage follows it. Drafts and trashed paintings
        stay put. The orange edge shows where the card lands; dropping it where
        it already sits does nothing.
      </p>
      <h3>When a buyer writes</h3>
      <p>
        When someone taps “Send inquiry” on a painting page, their note lands
        straight in your inbox — hit reply and it goes to them. Most buyers
        decide within a day, so a quick reply sells more paintings than a
        perfect one. Say whether pickup or shipping is on the table, with a
        rough cost. After it's paid and on its way, open the painting and mark
        it sold.
      </p>
      <h3>Your email list</h3>
      <p>
        The Email page shows who gets your emails. People join from the “Notify
        me” box on the site, or you can add someone you met — they get an email
        and join when they tap its link. Addresses stay blurred until you hover
        over or tap one, in case someone's looking over your shoulder.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Send email” writes to the whole list at once, for an open studio, a
        show, or anything else. The preview shows exactly what they'll get.
      </p>
      <h3>Homepage banner</h3>
      <p>
        One note on the homepage at a time — what you type is what buyers see.
        It ends on its own, so a forgotten one doesn't linger. Saving replaces
        the one showing now; Remove hides it.
      </p>
      <h3>QR codes for shows</h3>
      <p>
        The QR codes page has a card for each painting. Print them and stand one
        beside its painting: a phone camera opens that painting's page, where
        people can ask about it later or see it on their own wall at home.
      </p>
      <h3>Metrics</h3>
      <p>
        How many people visited in the past 30 days, which paintings they looked
        at, and how they found you. “Direct” means they came straight to your
        site — typing the address, a bookmark, a QR code, or a link in a text or
        app. Your own visits to the studio aren't counted.
      </p>
      <h3>If something looks wrong</h3>
      <p>
        A painting disappeared? Check the Trash fold on the Collection page —
        deleted paintings rest there for 30 days and come back in one tap.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Your sign-in has run out” means the day-long sign-in ended. Reload the
        page and enter the code from your email — whatever you were typing is
        kept.
      </p>
      <p>
        “Saving is paused” means GitHub, where the site keeps your paintings,
        turned the site away. Nothing you've saved is lost — tell Chris.
      </p>
      <h3>Shipping and getting paid</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="https://chitchats.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Chit Chats
          </a>{" "}
          — cheapest tracked Canada/US shipping.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="https://www.pirateship.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
            Pirate Ship
          </a>{" "}
          — backup for US labels.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Getting paid: start with e-transfer. When a buyer writes back, send them
        your e-transfer address and hold the painting until the money lands — we
        can set up a checkout thing later if we want.
      </p>
      <p>
        GST: you don't charge it until your sales pass $30,000 in a year (any
        four quarters in a row). You're well under that, so the listed price is
        what the buyer pays. If you get close, tell Chris and we'll register and
        switch GST on.
      </p>
      <details>
        <summary>Advanced Settings</summary>
        <p class="hint">
          Email list <span id="flag-email">…</span> · phone alerts for buyer
          notes <span id="flag-push">…</span> · posting to social media{" "}
          <span id="flag-social">…</span>
        </p>
        <label>
          Local API token (only needed on this device if Access is off)
          <input id="admin-token" type="password" autocomplete="off" />
        </label>
        <p class="hint">
          This browser can keep working offline: <span id="cap-sw">…</span> ·
          sending in the background: <span id="cap-sync">…</span> · network:{" "}
          <span id="cap-net">…</span>
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>
  </main>
</Layout>

<script src="../../components/AdminPanel.ts"></script>

<style>
  #sec-info ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0.75rem 0 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
  }
  /* The drawer animates in script, since interpolate-size is
     Chromium-only. These are the opacity end states only. A CSS
     transition here would fight the script. */
  /* Clicking the drawer header shouldn't select its text. */
  #sec-info summary {
    -webkit-user-select: none;
    user-select: none;
  }
  #sec-info details > :not(summary) {
    opacity: 0;
  }
  #sec-info details[open] > :not(summary) {
    opacity: 1;
  }
</style>
